title: Completion views
tags: Commands
kind: reference
audience: dev
aliases: completion completions candidates views
summary: A candidate of a question is a label and a value; a completion may name its candidates in several ways, cycled with a key, and fetches one bounded page at a time.

The candidates offered by a question in the command input
([[Interactive command arguments]], or a script's prompt) follow three rules.

!! A candidate is a label and a value

The ''label'' is what the list shows and what the typed text is matched against; the
''value'' is what the command receives. A plain string is both, so a completion with
nothing to gain from the split stays a plain list. Where they differ, the list names
things the way a person reads them while the command gets what it must act on: a
`ref` candidate is labelled by its target's path or name, and valued with its uuid
([[Ref value seeds]]).

Text typed in full that spells a label names its value, exactly as picking the row
does. Text no label spells is taken as itself — which is how a uuid, or a value that
does not exist yet, is entered.

!! No two rows read the same

Two candidates with the same label and different values are both suffixed with their
value, for every completion whatever it completes: no row is a coin flip between two
things, and each can still be reached by typing it whole. Exact duplicates — same
label, same value — are merged first.

!! Views

A completion may offer several ''views'' of its candidates: the same things, named
differently. Usually there is one, and nothing shows. A `ref` value has several,
because which naming reaches a metarecord fastest depends on the metarecord — its
path, its name, a label.

An argument declares its views as an array, each with a `title` and an `items`
function. <<key "completion:cycle forward" "ctrl+,">> and
<<key "completion:cycle back" "ctrl+.">> walk the array, wrapping; the hint at the
right of the line shows the title and the position (`path 1/3`). The default keys
are chords on purpose: a plain comma stays typable. With one view the command does
nothing.

!! One page at a time

A view's function is called with the text typed and the arguments collected so far,
and returns one ''page'' of candidates — an array, or `{items, more}` where `more`
says the source holds more than it returned.

* A complete page is narrowed by the local filter alone, as the user types.
* A truncated page is asked again with the new text, 150 ms after the last keystroke.

So a view never fetches more than one bounded page — one daemon request, with a
limit and the view's own order — however many candidates stand behind it. The first
page is requested when the question opens and is not waited for.

The rules themselves — normalising a candidate, the duplicate-label suffix — are in
`panel-shim/completions.js` (served as `/__completions.js`), shared by the shell and
the panels that have to resolve a typed label the same way.
